# Observability Incident Response

Loom exposes three primary observability domains: **crash**, **crons**, and **sessions**.

## 1. Triage crash signals

```bash
loom crash projects --org <org-id>
loom crash issues --project <project-id>
loom crash api-keys --project <project-id>
```

Common escalation path:

```bash
loom crash create-project --org <org-id> --name "api" --platform rust
loom crash create-api-key --project <project-id> --name ci-capture --key-type capture
```

## 2. Monitor scheduled job health with crons

```bash
loom crons monitors --org <org-id>
loom crons get --org <org-id> --slug <monitor-slug>
loom crons stats --org <org-id> --slug <monitor-slug> --period week
```

Create/update/pause/resume:

```bash
loom crons create --org <org-id> --slug billing-sync --name "Billing Sync" --cron "*/5 * * * *"
loom crons pause --org <org-id> --slug billing-sync
loom crons resume --org <org-id> --slug billing-sync
```

## 3. Review session health

```bash
loom sessions list --project <project-id>
loom sessions releases --project <project-id> --environment production --days 7
loom sessions release --project <project-id> --version <release-version>
```

## 4. Use ping URLs for external checks

Server routes support ping status updates:

- `GET/POST /ping/{key}`
- `GET /ping/{key}/start`
- `GET /ping/{key}/fail`

These are typically called from cron jobs or external schedulers.

<Callout variant="warning" title="Response ownership">
	When an incident involves shared code context, link the operational artifact to a Loom thread and set visibility explicitly.
</Callout>
